Description: qtwidgets: Give widgets their depth, and move parts per eye
 .
 When the style draws a widget's own element, the element gives the widget
 its depth, relative to the nearest widget around it that has one, fitted
 into the limits per window as in Qt Quick. The widget carries its parallax
 as _q_stereo_parallax: whoever draws the window once per eye moves the
 whole widget by its share, its text included, and cuts a widget behind
 the one it lies on to its own rectangle. A hidden widget takes no place.
 .
 Parts drawn inside one widget, such as the current tab of a tab bar, are
 moved by the style itself while an eye is drawn, which the window's
 _q_stereo_eye says, and a part behind its widget is cut to its own
 rectangle.
 .
 Frames now carry their QFrame::Shadow as the hints raised and sunken, so
 a style can give a frame Qt's own "the frame and contents appear raised"
 or "sunken" as a depth. Breeze matches neither, so nothing changes there.
 .
 In a window that shows no depth nothing is recorded and nothing moves.
